        "text": "Geopolitics is back to fragile deal-risk from war panic as the US-Iran framework moved forward.\n\n• US-Iran: Trump rejected reports of a finalized deal, saying the US is still “not satisfied,” while Rubio said talks made progress but remain unfinished.\n\n• Hormuz: framework talks aim to reopen the Strait, but Washington rejects any single-country control and Iran still wants leverage over transit terms.\n\n• Sanctions: Trump ruled out sanctions relief for Tehran, denying reports that Iran would get immediate economic relief in exchange for uranium concessions.\n\n• Trump: accused Tehran of stalling and trying to “out-wait” him until the US midterms, framing Iran as negotiating from weakness; also rejected the idea of Russia or China taking possession of Iran’s highly enriched uranium.\n\n• Military risk: US strikes near Bandar Abbas, Iran’s MQ-9 drone downing claim, and renewed Hezbollah/Lebanon escalation kept the war premium alive.\n\n• LNG/energy: Canada-Germany LNG cooperation and India/Indonesia Russian crude flows show global energy routes keep shifting around sanctions and conflict risk.\n\n• Ukraine/Russia: Kyiv urgently seeks more Patriot defenses as Russia signals Oreshnik missile pressure and UK intel flags heavy Russian war losses, while Rubio says Washington remains ready to mediate.\n\n• Israel-Gaza/Lebanon: Israeli escalation in Lebanon and diplomatic backlash around Gaza flotilla detentions added another regional pressure layer.\n\n• Markets: stocks split between AI/US macro support and renewed geopolitics; oil and inflation risk are now back as the key macro transmission channel.\n\nIt now looks like a fragile framework where every unresolved item can become the next market shock: uranium, sanctions, Hormuz control, oil flows, or another military incident.\n\nIs this finally the road to diplomacy, or just more “cards” to shake the global markets?",
